{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "זקן ויראת", :translit "zaken v'yareita", :gloss "an elder and you shall fear"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "The verse's command to fear the elder is read as a reciprocal obligation: the elder must not impose undue burden on others."}], :frame {:text "Kiddushin 32b derives from the words 'and you shall fear your God' (Leviticus 19:32) that an elder should not cause others to trouble themselves on his behalf, since the verse implies divine scrutiny of the elder's conduct.", :sources ["Kiddushin 32b"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מפני שיבה תקום", :translit "mipnei seivah takum", :gloss "before the aged you shall rise"} {:he "וכי יגור אתך גר", :translit "v'chi yagur it'cha ger", :gloss "and when a stranger resides with you"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut (adjacency): the verse about rising before the aged is immediately followed by the verse about the stranger, enabling a derash."}], :frame {:text "Kiddushin 32b interprets 'זקן' (aged) as 'זה קנה חכמה' — one who has acquired wisdom. However, Kiddushin 33a states that one must rise even before an aged person who is not a scholar, drawing from the obligation in Leviticus 19:32. The Ba'al HaTurim uses the adjacency of the stranger-verse to extend the obligation even to an elder lacking wisdom.", :sources ["Kiddushin 32b" "Kiddushin 33a" "Leviticus 19:32"]}} {:quotes [{:he "אני ה'", :translit "ani Hashem", :gloss "I am YHWH"} {:he "וכי יגור אתך גר", :translit "v'chi yagur it'cha ger", :gloss "and when a stranger resides with you"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut (adjacency): the phrase 'I am YHWH' closes the preceding verse and is immediately followed by the stranger-verse, equating the two."}], :frame {:text "The Talmud states that one who oppresses a ger (stranger/convert) violates a prohibition multiple times, and the midrashic tradition equates harm to a stranger with harm to God Himself, based on the repeated phrase 'I am YHWH' adjacent to the commandments regarding the stranger.", :sources ["Bava Metzia 59b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "The verse's command to fear the elder is read as a reciprocal obligation: the elder must not impose undue burden on others.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Semikhut (adjacency): the verse about rising before the aged is immediately followed by the verse about the stranger, enabling a derash.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Semikhut (adjacency): the phrase 'I am YHWH' closes the preceding verse and is immediately followed by the stranger-verse, equating the two.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:24:35.770223385Z", :model "glm-5.2"}